okay in this lesson we're going to talk  about the pythagorean theorem and  pythagorean triples so just a refresher  when you have a right triangle okay the  two sides that make up the right angle  those are called the legs or the sides  of the triangle and the side that's  across from the right angle that's the  hypotenuse that's called c we have a  formula a squared plus b squared equals  c squared so the sum of the  leg squared equals the hypotenuse  squared and the pythagorean triples are  ones that come up oftentimes on the act  because they're they're easy to work  with and they're it's also a time saver  if you know these ones by by memory so  basically if the two legs were three and  four automatically know the hypotenuse  is five or if you knew that one of the  legs is three and the hypotenuse is five  then you know the other leg is 4. so  these are common ones that come up a lot  it's definitely  useful to memorize those and the other  thing too is that you can recognize is  that multiples of these are also going  to be right triangles so if i saw like a  6 8 i would automatically know that the  missing side here is going to be 10. all  i did was double all those so if you can  recognize that it's a multiple of one of  the pythagorean triples as well that  again is just a time saver but you don't  have to know that you can always just go  back to the pythagorean theorem and  solve for the missing the missing side  so let's go through a couple examples  and get some practice see if you can do  these on your own and we'll go through  them together so this first example  you know a person's flying a kite let's  just say that they're standing on the  string here okay with their foot and  here's the kite  they let out 130 feet of string uh it's  120 feet okay horizontally to below the  kite and we're trying to find the height  of the kite so how would you do a  problem like that  okay so what we could do is we could  just do the pythagorean theorem we could  say a squared  plus b squared  equals  c squared  and then all we have to do is say x  squared equals 130 squared minus 120  squared and then just take the square  root of both sides after we simplify and  that's going to be  the height but an easier way to do this  problem if we recognize c 12  13 it looks like this pythagorean triple  right here this side must be 5 but you  can see these have been multiplied by 10  so this height is going to be 50. so  it's a little bit of a shortcut you can  see it's 50 feet above the ground okay  let's try example number two now this  one's just more straightforward but just  to practice  they're giving you the two legs and  you're solving for the hypotenuse so how  would you do that one  well let's see we've got a squared  plus b squared  equals c squared so this is 36 plus 100  equals x squared we have 136 equals x  squared we take the square root of both  sides and you want to be able to  simplify these radicals so let's see if  we can break down 136 so i'm just going  to go to my calculator real quick  let's see half of 136  is let's see 68. so we've got 68 times 2  we've got 34 times 2 we have 2 times 17  and when you're taking the square root  you know you're looking for a pair of  the same  quantity so we have a pair of 2's that's  4 the square root of 4 is 2  and then we have a 17 times 2 left over  so that's  2 square root of 34. so you can either  get a exact answer like this or you  could do your just get a decimal  approximation so the square root of 136  would be about 11.7  is this length right here  so definitely pythagorean theorem is  important to know for the act and again  a little shortcut here with the  pythagorean triples
 

